WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.029
Many actors look back on certain movies without
fondness, thanks to on-set problems, disappointing

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:08.540
scripts, or any number of other problems.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.500
Here are just a few who regretted some of
their roles, movies, and decisions.

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:19.320
The Help told the story of several maids and
the families they worked for in 1960s Mississippi.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.360
Starring a packed cast of talented actresses
including Viola Davis, Octavia Spencer, and

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.300
Emma Stone, the film chronicled a white woman's
mission to publish a book told from the perspective

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.460
of Mississippi maids.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.020
Even though Davis received her second Oscar
nomination for the role, she has since admitted

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.740
that she has reservations about her role in
The Help.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.770
Davis has never been shy about speaking her
mind, and for a film that was supposed to

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.360
support those without a voice, she eventually
decided that it fell short.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.940
The whole experience seems best summed up
by her response to Empire when asked about

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.820
what it was like to wear a fat suit:

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.120
"I like playing someone other than myself.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.240
It's only in hindsight that I go 'oh God,
why did I do that?'"

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.820
Anyone who's ever opened a newspaper is familiar
with Garfield, a surly orange cat who loves

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.460
lasagna and hates Mondays.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:10.720
Most avid moviegoers are familiar with Bill
Murray, the comedy legend who starred in famous

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.300
films like Ghostbusters, Caddyshack, and Groundhog's
Day.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.920
When Garfield: The Movie was released in 2004,
it baffled Murray's fans as to why the normally

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.270
picky actor chose Garfield as one of his projects.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:28.180
As it turned out, Murray apparently assumed
that the writer of the film, Joel Cohen, was

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.590
one half of writer-director duo the Coen brothers,
and Murray wanted to appear in one of their

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:33.590
films.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.979
"You know, I think I had a nightmare like
this once.”

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:42.950
The Fifty Shades book series became international
bestsellers, so popular that it basically

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.719
guaranteed an eventual movie adaptation.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:50.960
Once it was unsurprisingly announced, Jamie
Dornan and Dakota Johnson beat off stiff competition

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.289
to play the leads.

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:54.930
That doesn't mean they were happy with their
success, though.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:00.579
Dornan notably hated the entire series and
was fairly unsubtle about the entire thing—even

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:04.479
while on a promotional tour for the final
movie, Dornan couldn't resist complaining

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.009
about watching the final cut to Jimmy Kimmel:

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:09.690
"By the end, I'm literally watching it like
this.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.290
I can't bear it."

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.650
Dakota Johnson was only slightly more subtle
about how much she didn't enjoy having to

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:19.159
act in the movies—or having to pretend to
be close to Dornan.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:21.680
While promoting Fifty Shades Darker, she told
Today:

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.750
"I think we were kind of forced into being
best friends."

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:33.010
Upon its release in 1984, the original Terminator
film became an instant classic, cementing

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.769
Arnold Schwarzenegger as a movie star and
launching the career of director James Cameron.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:42.950
The second film installment, 1991's Terminator
2: Judgment Day, was an even bigger success

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.880
than the first, thanks to its astounding visual
effects and exciting action sequences, even

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.720
winning multiple Academy Awards.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.599
Viewers demanded more adventures within this
action-packed universe, and studio execs have

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:59.110
been only too happy to comply with an entire
franchise which has spanned decades and has

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.800
included sequels, comic books, and a television
series.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.739
2015's Terminator Genisys was the fifth installment
in the series — meant as a "soft reboot,"

00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:11.470
it featured Schwarzenegger's return to the
franchise.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.300
What followed was some a poor box office performance
and a terrible reception from critics, and

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.500
its planned sequel was eventually canceled
— which definitely came as a relief to star

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.530
Emilia Clarke, best known as Daenerys Targaryen
on Game of Thrones.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:29.379
In fact, Clarke later told Vanity Fair that
"no one had a good time," not even director

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:34.209
Alan Taylor (with whom Clarke frequently worked
on Thrones).

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.269
Based on an idea conceived by Will Smith,
After Earth told the tale of a father and

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:42.239
son in the distant future, played by Smith
and his real-life son Jaden.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:46.780
The two characters spend their time leading
space expeditions to find inhabitable planets

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.769
and accidentally land on a damaged, dangerous
Earth.

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:54.299
As the two are separated and struggle to survive
on an unfamiliar and hostile plane, audiences

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:59.120
follow the journey of Jaden's character as
he struggles to reunite with his father.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.480
Despite the stars' best efforts, the film
flopped at the box office and performed similarly

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.940
poorly with critics.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.650
In the aftermath of After Earth, Smith admitted
that the film was was "the most painful failure"

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:14.329
of his career which is strong words for an
actor that starred in Wild, Wild West.

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:18.600
"When you're telling this story to your grandkids,
you make sure you leave this part out."

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.150
"Don't worry."

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:24.330
There was once a time when few actors would
even consider turning down an opportunity

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.720
to work with Woody Allen.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.130
He's a multiple Academy Award-winner, and
movies like Annie Hall, Hannah and her Sisters,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:33.060
and Crimes and Misdemeanors are widely viewed
as classics.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.720
However, after his controversial marriage
to Soon-Yi Previn, the adopted daughter of

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:43.970
his former partner Mia Farrow, Allen's personal
life often took over headlines; in 2014, Dylan

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:48.770
Farrow, one of Mia Farrow's other adopted
children, repeated her allegations of sexual

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.680
abuse at the hands of the famous director.

00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:55.400
With the rise of the #MeToo movement, Allen
became a public relations nightmare for the

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.800
actors and artists who chose to work with
him, and they started speaking out against

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.320
him in droves.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:04.830
Two notable examples were Greta Gerwig and
Ellen Page, who appeared in 2012's To Rome

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:05.830
With Love.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.940
Both actors have spoken out against everything
from gender inequality in film to their own

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.940
experiences with assault, and they didn't
mince words looking back on their time on

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.651
the set with Allen — Gerwig has said she
wouldn't work with the director again, while

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.160
Page called it the "biggest regret" of her
career.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:27.080
Years before the Netflix television show,
there was another Daredevil live-action adaptation.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.620
2003's Daredevil starred Ben Affleck as the
blind vigilante, and Jennifer Garner as Elektra,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.580
another superhero and Daredevil's love interest.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.919
On the villain side of things were Colin Farrell
as brutal assassin Bullseye, and Michael Clarke

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.360
Duncan as the vicious crime lord known as
Kingpin.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:47.180
Though the film performed well at the box
office, it was panned by critics, and remains

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.800
an infamous example of one of the worst comic-to-film
adaptations in the modern era.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.900
Affleck, whose career has seen plenty of highs
and lows, clearly agrees, as he admitted in

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.990
2013 that he "actually regrets" Daredevil
despite loving the character.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.240
He cited that earlier failure as one moody
vigilante as the reason why he'd agree to

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.980
star as another—this time for DC as the
new Batman.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:14.280
It's a little crazy to think that the actor
who also starred in Gigli could have an even

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.060
bigger regret, but clearly, Daredevil's botched
big-screen debut still weighs on Affleck.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:25.520
Sometimes, a superhero movie can catapult
an actor to stardom even if they're already

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.350
well-known, like when Robert Downey Jr. breathed
new life into his career by stepping into

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:30.840
Tony Stark's Iron Man suit.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.900
Other times, however, that same opportunity
can become an actor's biggest regret.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:40.130
Even for an actor like George Clooney, who
seems impervious to professional misfortune,

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.580
a bad superhero film can become a blight on
any performer's career.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:49.400
Clooney's time as the Caped Crusader in 1997's
Batman &amp; Robin certainly proves it.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.060
Directed by Joel Schumacher, it's become a
long-running joke, is routinely called one

00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:58.701
of the worst films of all time, and is the
lowest-grossing live-action Batman movie to

00:06:58.701 --> 00:07:03.760
date, shutting down the entire franchise until
its 2005 reboot, Batman Begins.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:08.669
Clooney's career didn't suffer any serious
decline after Batman &amp; Robin — he's won

00:07:08.669 --> 00:07:13.900
two Academy Awards, become a lauded director,
and even received the AFI Lifetime Achievement

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.320
Award in 2018.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.600
Still, he told Deadline in 2013 that he keeps
a photo of himself as Batman in his office

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.220
as a reminder of what happens when you make
a movie for purely commercial reasons.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.030
As the self-made star of Rocky, Sylvester
Stallone had his pick of projects for years,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:35.849
since he was known as an actor capable of
handling action scenes just as easily as serious,

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:36.849
dramatic moments.

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:41.889
Still, he was never exactly known for his
comedy, and he shocked audiences by taking

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:44.919
a light-hearted detour with 1992's Stop!

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:45.720
Or My Mom Will Shoot.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.360
The film was an odd buddy-cop outing that
paired Stallone with Estelle Getty, best known

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.780
for her role as Sophia on The Golden Girls.

00:07:53.349 --> 00:07:58.319
The two banded together as a mother and son
team to solve crimes — a flimsy premise

00:07:58.319 --> 00:08:01.289
that was met with predictably terrible reviews.

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:05.409
The script for the film was infamously bad
— so bad that it was hard not to wonder

00:08:05.409 --> 00:08:07.729
what Stallone saw in the project.

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:12.000
Arnold Schwarzenegger later took the blame,
admitting that he feigned an interest in starring

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.280
in the film to trick his action hero rival
into taking the role and harpooning his own

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:16.800
career.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.380
For his part, Stallone has made it clear that
he's far from fond of this film, calling it

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:25.320
"the worst" of his career and admitting that
he regrets making it in the first place.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:31.840
Released in 1993 and based on one of the most
popular video games of all time, Super Mario

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:34.900
Bros. set an unfortunate standard for video
game films.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:40.200
The film was a complete failure both critically
and commercially, in large part thanks to

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.300
its flimsy plot and terrible writing.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.000
However, the actors involved certainly weren't
terrible; Super Mario Bros. starred the late

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.580
Bob Hoskins as Mario and John Leguizamo as
his brother Luigi, with a plot revolving around

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.150
a scenario that mimicked the game — Mario
and Luigi had to find and rescue Princess

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.830
Peach from the evil King Koopa.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:03.570
Hoskins, an accomplished and well-regarded
British actor best known for Mona Lisa and

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:09.400
Who Framed Roger Rabbit, has made no secret
of his disdain for the film — during a Q&amp;A

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:13.940
in 2011, he made it clear that the project
was the biggest regret of his entire career.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:18.390
To his credit, Leguizamo felt the same way,
but was kinder about it, praising the rest

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:22.090
of the cast and crew for doing their best
in a bad situation.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:26.960
Stephenie Meyer's Twilight book series was
an immediate hit when the first installment

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.280
arrived on shelves in 2005.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.810
The books tell the story of Bella Swan, a
normal teenager who unexpectedly falls in

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.520
love with a century-old vampire, Edward Cullen,
at her high school.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.720
A film franchise followed in 2008 thanks to
overwhelming demand, with Kristen Stewart

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.250
as Bella and Robert Pattinson as Edward.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:51.510
The series grossed nearly $3.3 billion at
the box office, creating a pop culture phenomenon

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:52.830
for years to come.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:57.710
Still, throughout the series, the young actors
involved seemed displeased with their involvement,

00:09:57.710 --> 00:10:01.500
and have treated their experience with the
saga with polite distaste in the intervening

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:02.020
years.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:06.570
Pattinson was by far the most vocal about
his hatred for the series; over the years,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:11.640
he's called Edward a prude, dissed Meyer's
ideas, and made it clear that he hated starring

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.980
in the movies more than anybody else involved.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:18.380
"A lot of things in the Twilight world doesn't
make sense.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:20.860
Why are they still going to high school?

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.140
They're 100 years old."

